<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamal Abdel Nasser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nasser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamal Abdel Nasser was an Egyptian army officer who led the Free Officers' coup of 1952 and became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1956. He nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1956, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> and pursued Arab socialism. The leading figure of pan-Arab nationalism, he headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1958 to 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gamal Abdel Nasser Hussein was born in Bakos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, on 15 January 1918, the son of a postal worker from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. His family moved often, which showed him the class divisions of Egyptian society, and he read widely, including biographies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, Atatürk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bismarck-battleship">Bismarck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>. In 1937 his police record of anti-government protest at first blocked his entry to the Royal Military Academy; he briefly studied law before reapplying. His first battlefield experience was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in the 1948 Arab–Israeli War. On his return he began organising dissenting officers. In 1944 he had married Tahia Kazem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 25 January 1952 about 7,000 British soldiers attacked the police station in Ismailia, killing 50 Egyptian policemen, and the Cairo Fire riots followed. Nasser published a six-point programme to dismantle feudalism and British influence. In January 1953 he overcame Mohamed Naguib's opposition and banned all political parties in favour of the Liberation Rally. After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> member fired eight shots at him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 26 October 1954, he cracked down on the Brotherhood, placed Naguib under house arrest and assumed executive office; he was formally elected president in June 1956. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung-conference">Bandung Conference</a> in April 1955 he was treated as the leading representative of the Arab countries.</p><p>On 19 July 1956 the US and UK withdrew their offer to finance the Aswan Dam, and Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company. The UK, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Israel secretly agreed to take the canal and topple him, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> ended in what was seen as his political victory, and his prestige across the Arab world rose. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> radio station, the Voice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, spread his ideas of Arab unity. From 1961 he nationalised much of the economy, and the National Charter of 1962 promised universal health care, affordable housing and wider women's rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s intervention in North Yemen from 1962 cost 26,000 Egyptian soldiers, and Nasser later called it a miscalculation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 led Nasser to resign, but demonstrations brought him back; he named himself prime minister and began the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">Attrition</a> in January 1968. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> summit closed on 28 September 1970 he suffered a heart attack and died at 52. His funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> drew five to six million mourners. Supporters credit him with making Egypt independent of British influence and widening access to housing, education and health services; critics point to authoritarianism and the dominance of the military over civil institutions. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> later departed from Nasserist policies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gamal Abdel Nasser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 22–23 July 1952 the Free Officers Movement, led by Muhammad Naguib and <b>Gamal Abdel Nasser</b>, seized power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The NLF also fought a rival nationalist group, the Front for the Liberation of Occupied South Yemen (FLOSY), backed by <b>Nasser</b>’s Egypt, and by 1967 it had defeated FLOSY and taken over most of the protectorate states as their rulers fled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1956 Tito, Nehru and <b>Nasser</b> signed the Declaration of Brijuni in Yugoslavia, and in September 1961, on Tito’s initiative, the first Conference of Heads of State or Government of Non-Aligned Countries met in Belgrade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Nyerere of Tanzania, Sékou Touré of Guinea, Modibo Keïta of Mali, <b>Gamal Abdel Nasser</b> of Egypt, Haile Selassie of Ethiopia and Kenneth Kaunda of Zambia shaped the OAU.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Ba'athism sympathised with the non-aligned politics of Nehru, <b>Nasser</b> and Tito and opposed affiliation with either the Western or the Eastern bloc in the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/baathism">Ba'athism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1956 <b>Gamal Abdel Nasser</b> of Egypt began a “state feminism” that outlawed discrimination by gender and gave women the vote but blocked feminist leaders’ political activism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bakos, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.24,29.96&amp;year=1951" aria-label="Bakos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gamal Abdel Nasser"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 4894h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 4894h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.24,29.96&amp;year=1951">What was here in 1951? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
